Full Process: Deploy Your Own Code to S3 via CodePipeline
🪣 Step 1: Create an S3 Bucket

Go to AWS Console → S3 → Create bucket

Enter a unique name — example:
my-portfolio-site-demo

Uncheck “Block all public access” → Confirm

Click Create bucket

🌍 Step 2: Enable Static Website Hosting

Open your new bucket

Go to Properties → Static website hosting

Enable it:

Index document: index.html

Error document: error.html (optional)

Click Save changes

Copy the Website endpoint URL (keep it safe)

💻 Step 3: Upload Your Own Code to GitHub

If you already have your own HTML/CSS/JS project, follow this:

Create a new repository in GitHub (example: mywebsite)

On your local system, open a terminal in your project folder
Example folder: C:\Users\You\Desktop\mywebsite

Run these commands:

git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/mywebsite.git
git push -u origin main


Your code is now stored in GitHub.

⚙️ Step 4: Create the Pipeline

Go to AWS Console → CodePipeline → Create pipeline

Name it something like MyWebsitePipeline

Leave “Service Role” as New Service Role

Click Next

🔗 Step 5: Choose Source

(This is the screen in your image)

Source provider: Select GitHub (via GitHub App)

Click Connect to GitHub

Authorize AWS to access your account

Choose your repository (mywebsite)

Choose your branch (main)

Click Next

🧱 Step 6: Build Stage

Since your website is static (HTML/CSS/JS):

Click Skip build stage

Confirm “Yes, skip build stage”

Click Next

📦 Step 7: Deploy Stage

Deploy provider: Amazon S3

Region: Choose same region as your bucket

Bucket name: Select your bucket (my-portfolio-site-demo)

Extract file before deploy: ✅ (check this)

Click Next

🧾 Step 8: Review and Create

Review all stages → click Create pipeline

AWS will:

Pull your code from GitHub

Deploy it to your S3 bucket automatically

🌐 Step 9: Verify Deployment

Go to your S3 bucket → Properties → Static website hosting

Copy your Website endpoint

Paste it in browser → You’ll see your own HTML/CSS site live!

🔁 Step 10: Automatic Updates

Now whenever you:

Edit your code locally,

Commit and push it to GitHub,

CodePipeline automatically runs → redeploys the updated site to S3 🎉

⚠️ Optional: Make Public Access Work

If you get a “403 Forbidden” error, add this bucket policy (replace bucket name):

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "PublicReadGetObject",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-portfolio-site-demo/*"
    }
  ]
}


Go to Bucket → Permissions → Bucket Policy → Paste this → Save.
